  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Massachusetts received $15.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Contract activity included 86,981 awards with an average award size of $182K. Obligations in Massachusetts were down 18.0% year over year. The Department of Defense dominated buying activity, obligating $12.4B, while the next-largest buyers were the Department of Veterans Affairs with $858M and the Department of Transportation with $743M. Contractors tied to defense and aerospace captured the largest shares: RAYTHEON COMPANY appears multiple times on the vendor list with awards of $3.4B, $1.2B, $970M, and $507M. Industry concentration focused on aerospace, guided missile and space vehicle manufacturing, and engineering and R&D services. Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing led industries with $3.2B. Historical totals show higher obligations in earlier years, including $24.2B in FY21 and $19.3B in FY25, which provides context for the current-year partial total.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove the year-over-year decline?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense remained the largest buyer at $12.4B but overall obligations fell 18.0% year over year. Defense and aerospace vendors still received large awards, yet the statewide total dropped to $15.9B in FY2026 to date from prior fiscal highs such as $24.2B in FY21 and $19.3B in FY25. Concentration in a few large vendors and industries made the statewide total sensitive to shifts in a small set of programs and awards."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate Massachusetts federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $12.4B, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $858M and the Department of Transportation at $743M. RAYTHEON COMPANY, GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY, and MASSACHUSETTS INSTITUTE OF TECHNOLOGY were the largest vendors by dollar value, with RAYTHEON COMPANY appearing at $3.4B, $1.2B, $970M, and $507M across multiple entries. Industry leaders included Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $3.2B and Engineering Services at $1.8B."
      }
    ]
  },
